WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
curl is widely used in Bash scripts because it connects automation to web services with very little ceremony.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:16.040
A short script can call an API, download a file, upload an artifact, check service health, fetch metadata, or trigger a deployment workflow.

3
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.780
That makes curl useful, but it also makes curl security-relevant.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.420
An HTTP request is an operational action.

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:32.760
It has a destination, method, headers, body, identity, output, status, and failure mode.

6
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:42.380
In a production script, those details may affect deployments, monitoring, release evidence, configuration, inventory, support bundles, or incident response workflows.

7
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.180
The risks are often ordinary engineering mistakes.

8
00:00:46.280 --> 00:01:02.440
A script may send data to the wrong URL, accept a response without checking status, leak a token in a log, follow a redirect to an unexpected destination, overwrite a file, or treat unvalidated response text as trusted input to the next step.

9
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.680
Secure curl usage means making HTTP automation intentional.

10
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:19.620
The script should define allowed endpoints, keep transport trust enabled, protect secrets, preserve argument boundaries, check results, and keep logs useful without exposing sensitive data.

11
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.420
The defensive goal is not to make curl hard to use.

12
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.880
The goal is scoped, observable HTTP automation.

13
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:35.620
When requests are explicit and reviewable, teams can use curl safely as Bash glue without turning small scripts into hidden trust decisions.
